{% extends 'layouts/base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}SWAT - Documentation - Customization{% endblock %}

{% block body %}

<main>

    <section class="documentation-customization-page">

        <div class="hero">
            <h1 class="hero_title">Customization</h1>
            <p class="hero_subtitle">
                Learn how to adapt SWAT to your specific needs and make it your own.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="section-title">
            <span class="section-title_text">Customization overview</span>
        </div>

        <div class="customization-intro">

            <p class="customization-intro_text">
                SWAT is designed as a flexible starting point for Symfony projects.
                The provided structure, frontend stack and CI/CD workflows can be adapted
                to match your own development and deployment workflow.
            </p>

            <p class="customization-intro_text">
                This section highlights the main areas where customization typically happens 
                when starting a new project based on SWAT.
            </p>

        </div>


        <div class="section-title">
            <span class="section-title_text">Removing example content</span>
        </div>

        <div class="customization-cards">

            <article class="customization-card">
                <h2 class="customization-card_title">Example pages</h2>
                <p class="customization-card_description">
                    The project includes example pages used to demonstrate the structure
                    and layout of the application. These can be removed or replaced
                    when starting a real project.
                </p>
            </article>

            <article class="customization-card">
                <h2 class="customization-card_title">Example assets</h2>
                <p class="customization-card_description">
                    Example SCSS modules, TypeScript scripts and images are provided
                    to illustrate the frontend structure. They can be replaced with
                    your own components and styles.
                </p>
            </article>

        </div>

        <div class="customization-intro">

            <p class="customization-intro_text">
                SWAT includes demonstration pages and example assets used to illustrate
                the project structure and verify that the stack works correctly.
            </p>

            <p class="customization-intro_text">
                When starting a real project, you may want to remove this example content
                and keep only the base structure.
            </p>

        </div>

        <div class="terminal-block" style="max-width: 1100px; margin: 2rem auto;">

            <div class="terminal-block_terminal">

                <div class="terminal-block_line">
                    <span class="terminal-block_text terminal-block_text--comment">
                        # These commands will remove example pages, assets and tests from the project
                    </span>
                </div>

                <div class="terminal-block_line">
                    <span class="terminal-block_text terminal-block_text--comment">
                        # Make sure to review the commands and adjust them if needed before running
                    </span>
                </div>

                <div class="terminal-block_line">
                    <span class="terminal-block_text terminal-block_text--comment">&nbsp;</span>
                </div>

                <div class="terminal-block_line">
                    <span class="terminal-block_text terminal-block_text--comment">
                        # On windows, run this :
                    </span>
                </div>

                <div class="terminal-block_line">
                    <span class="terminal-block_prompt">></span>
                    <span class="terminal-block_text">
                        .\scripts\cleanup\clean-example-files.ps1
                    </span>
                </div>

                <div class="terminal-block_line">
                    <span class="terminal-block_text terminal-block_text--comment">&nbsp;</span>
                </div>

                <div class="terminal-block_line">
                    <span class="terminal-block_text terminal-block_text--comment">
                        # On Linux or MacOS, run this :
                    </span>
                </div>

                <div class="terminal-block_line">
                    <span class="terminal-block_prompt">></span>
                    <span class="terminal-block_text">
                        ./scripts/cleanup/clean-example-files.sh
                    </span>
                </div>

            </div>

        </div>


        <div class="section-title">
            <span class="section-title_text">Adapting the CI/CD workflows</span>
        </div>

        <div class="customization-block">

            <p class="customization-block_text">
                SWAT provides a ready-to-use GitHub Actions pipeline for validation,
                preproduction deployment and production deployment.
            </p>

            <p class="customization-block_text">
                Depending on your infrastructure and workflow, you may want to adapt
                the branches, triggers or deployment steps used in the CI/CD configuration.
            </p>

        </div>


        <div class="section-title">
            <span class="section-title_text">Adapting the deployment configuration</span>
        </div>

        <div class="customization-block">

            <p class="customization-block_text">
                The deployment workflows rely on SSH connections and GitHub repository
                secrets to securely connect to the target servers.
            </p>

            <p class="customization-block_text">
                You may need to adjust server paths, SSH configuration or environment
                variables depending on your hosting environment.
            </p>

        </div>


        <div class="section-title">
            <span class="section-title_text">Adapting the frontend build</span>
        </div>

        <div class="customization-block">

            <p class="customization-block_text">
                SWAT uses Webpack Encore with SCSS and TypeScript to build frontend assets.
                This setup can be extended or replaced depending on your frontend requirements.
            </p>

            <p class="customization-block_text">
                Developers can freely modify the asset structure, add new modules or integrate
                additional tooling as needed.
            </p>

        </div>


        <div class="section-title">
            <span class="section-title_text">Next steps</span>
        </div>

        <div class="next-steps">

            <p class="next-steps_text">
                Continue exploring the documentation to better understand how SWAT works
                and how it can fit into your development workflow.
            </p>

            <div class="next-steps_actions">

                <a href="{{ path('app_documentation_ci_cd') }}" class="next-steps_button">
                    CI/CD Pipeline
                </a>

                <a href="{{ path('app_documentation_project_structure') }}" class="next-steps_button">
                    Project Structure
                </a>

            </div>

        </div>

    </section>

</main>

{% endblock %}